# serializer version: 1
# name: test_dry_run_starts_no_process[drain]
  '''
  Plan for devops infra node drain of example-org/infra at <tmp>/repo
    1. EXEC the fetch of origin into <tmp>/repo
         git -C <tmp>/repo fetch --quiet origin
    2. EXEC the default branch of origin and its commit
         git -C <tmp>/repo rev-parse refs/remotes/origin/HEAD --abbrev-ref refs/remotes/origin/HEAD
    3. EXEC the branch and the commit of the checkout
         git -C <tmp>/repo rev-parse HEAD --abbrev-ref HEAD
    4. EXEC the export of the default branch, unpacked and removed after the read
         git -C <tmp>/repo archive --format=tar '--output=<private temporary folder>/tree.tar' '<commit of the default branch>'
    5. EXEC the Ansible inventory of the export
         <tmp>/ansible/bin/ansible-inventory -i '<inventory>' --list
    6. EXEC the health of each node in service
         kubectl --context homelab-example --request-timeout=30s get nodes -o json
    7. EXEC the node of node-3.example.com before the first change
         kubectl --context homelab-example --request-timeout=30s get node '<node of node-3.example.com>' -o json
    8. EXEC the pods of node-3.example.com, for their grace periods and volumes
         kubectl --context homelab-example --request-timeout=30s get pods --all-namespaces --field-selector 'spec.nodeName=<node of node-3.example.com>' -o json
    9. EXEC the PersistentVolumeClaims
         kubectl --context homelab-example --request-timeout=30s get pvc --all-namespaces -o json
   10. EXEC the PersistentVolumes, for the volumes that only one node mounts
         kubectl --context homelab-example --request-timeout=30s get pv -o json
   11. EXEC the server dry run of the drain of node-3.example.com
         kubectl --context homelab-example --request-timeout=30s drain '<node of node-3.example.com>' --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data --timeout=30s --dry-run=server
   12. EXEC the drain of node-3.example.com, with the longer of 180 s and the longest grace period and 60 s
         kubectl --context homelab-example --request-timeout=30s drain '<node of node-3.example.com>' --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data --timeout=180s
   13. EXEC the node of node-3.example.com after the last step
         kubectl --context homelab-example --request-timeout=30s get node '<node of node-3.example.com>' -o json
   14. EXEC the health of each node in service
         kubectl --context homelab-example --request-timeout=30s get nodes -o json
   15. EXEC the node of node-4.example.com before the first change
         kubectl --context homelab-example --request-timeout=30s get node '<node of node-4.example.com>' -o json
   16. EXEC the pods of node-4.example.com, for their grace periods and volumes
         kubectl --context homelab-example --request-timeout=30s get pods --all-namespaces --field-selector 'spec.nodeName=<node of node-4.example.com>' -o json
   17. EXEC the PersistentVolumeClaims
         kubectl --context homelab-example --request-timeout=30s get pvc --all-namespaces -o json
   18. EXEC the PersistentVolumes, for the volumes that only one node mounts
         kubectl --context homelab-example --request-timeout=30s get pv -o json
   19. EXEC the server dry run of the drain of node-4.example.com
         kubectl --context homelab-example --request-timeout=30s drain '<node of node-4.example.com>' --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data --timeout=30s --dry-run=server
   20. EXEC the drain of node-4.example.com, with the longer of 180 s and the longest grace period and 60 s
         kubectl --context homelab-example --request-timeout=30s drain '<node of node-4.example.com>' --ignore-daemonsets --delete-emptydir-data --timeout=180s
   21. EXEC the node of node-4.example.com after the last step
         kubectl --context homelab-example --request-timeout=30s get node '<node of node-4.example.com>' -o json
   22. WRITE the audit record infra.node.drain
  One host at a time in the given order. The first failure stops the run and leaves the node as the step left it, cordoned after a drain. A node that was cordoned before stays cordoned after a restart or a reboot.
  
  '''
# ---
